{{wikipedia|dab=termination}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[terminationem]]'' (accusative of ''[[terminatio]]'').

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The process of [[terminating]] or the state of being [[terminate]]d; [[incomplete]] [[ending]].
# The process of [[fire|firing]] an [[employee]]; ending one's employment at a business for any reason.
# An [[end]] in [[time]]; a [[conclusion]].
# An end in [[space]]; an [[edge]] or [[limit]].
# An [[outcome]] or [[result]].
# The last part of a [[word]]; a [[suffix]].
# {{context|medical|lang=en}} An induced [[abortion]].
# {{context|obsolete|rare|lang=en}} A word, a term.
#* '''1599''', [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[w:Much Ado About Nothing|Much Ado About Nothing]]'', [[s:Much Ado About Nothing (Shakespeare)|Act 2 Scene 1]]
#*: She speaks [[poniard]]s, and every word stabs: if her breath were as terrible as her '''terminations''', there were no living near her; she would [[infect]] to the north star.

====Derived terms====
* [[extermination]]
* [[terminative]]
* [[terminative case]]

====Synonyms====
* (''process of terminating　or state of being terminated''): [[discontinuation]]
* (''end in time''): [[close]], [[conclusion]], [[end]], [[finale]], [[finish]], [[stop]]
* (''end in space''): [[border]], [[edge]], [[end]], [[limit]], [[lip]], [[rim]], [[tip]]
* (''outcome''): [[consequence]], [[outcome]], [[result]], [[upshot]]
* {{qualifier|medical}}: [[abortion]], [[induced abortion]]

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|process of terminating or state of being terminated}} [[continuation]]

